            "target_entity_instance": "R-squared",
            "target_entity_type": "Metric",
            "paragraph": "The Linear mixed-effects model (LMM) incorporates both fixed effects (urban and climatic indicators) and random effects (district-level heterogeneity), achieving a strong fit (R\u00b2 = 0.961). The Linear mixed-effects model (LMM) fit was assessed using RMSE, MAE, MSE, and R\u00b2. The Linear mixed-effects model (LMM) performance was evaluated using standard metrics, including the coefficient of determination (R\u00b2). Further, the R-squared (R\u00b2) value of 0.961 highlights the model's exceptional explanatory power. An R\u00b2 of 0.961 implies that approximately 96.1% of the variance in the observed groundwater level data is explained by the model's predictors (population, built-up area, rainfall, and seasonal GWL dynamics) along with district-level random effects. Among the four districts (Table 4), Khorda exhibits the best model performance, with the highest R\u00b2 value of 0.98. Patna also demonstrates strong model performance, with an R\u00b2 of 0.96. The model still performs well in Ranchi, reflected in a high R\u00b2 of 0.97. In contrast, Kolkata records the lowest R\u00b2 value (0.93).",
